{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792230866,"nonce":"C+IdgqQPXRz148utpu+faI+WB+A=","title":"Python: Num beyond locals, in parameters, results, fields and list elements","message":"A local that holds two of int, float, bool and None is a Num, an unboxed (tag, int, float) value struct. Everything else joins Int and Float to Object: a parameter passed both, a result returning both, a field assigned both, a list of both. That boxing is the root of chaos (a42aae4: GVector fields), spectral-norm (bdddb0b: partial_sum starts at 0 and accumulates floats; u starts as [1] * N), and part of raytrace (Point and Vector fields from Vector(0, 0, 1) and float arithmetic).\n\nPlan, in steps that each land alone:\nA. Parameters and results: argument types and returns are read with expr_num and joined with join_num, so two numeric kinds make a Num; closures keep boxed parameters (they are called through records).\nB. Fields: a Num field stored as three scalar slots (tag, int, float) rather than a nested struct, which the backend's nested aggregate reads do not handle (project note on nested field access); dynamic getattr/setattr box and unbox through zb_num_box and the Num coercion.\nC. List elements: Elem::Num with its own list functions and box hooks, and a fresh list literal assigned to a local of List(Num) built as one, so `u = [1] * N` then `u = f(u)` keeps one element kind.\n\nStep A measured on its own (Mac, light build, retired instructions of one trial per kernel, base b544f755): total 287.46G -\u003e 288.38G; raytrace 0.968, meteor 0.961, scimark_lu 0.976; telco, fft, sparsematmult and float repeat within 1.5% either way. All 22 kernel checksums match CPython.","files":null}]}